straints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":15,"beg":{"line":85,"col":4},"end":{"line":85,"col":18}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" The lower bound of the range (inclusive)."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"start","ty":{"Deduplicated":14142}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":15,"beg":{"line":88,"col":4},"end":{"line":88,"col":16}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" The upper bound of the range (exclusive)."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"name":"end","ty":{"Deduplicated":14142}}]},"layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":12,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["ptr",0]},{"Ident":["non_null",0]},{"Ident":["NonNull",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":30,"beg":{"line":74,"col":0},"end":{"line":74,"col":35}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" `*mut T` but non-zero and [covariant]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is often the correct thing to use when building data structures using"},{"DocComment":" raw pointers, but is ultimately more dangerous to use because of its additional"},{"DocComment":" properties. If you're not sure if you should use `NonNull<T>`, just use `*mut T`!"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Unlike `*mut T`, the pointer must always be non-null, even if the pointer"},{"DocComment":" is never dereferenced. This is so that enums may use this forbidden value"},{"DocComment":" as a discriminant -- `Option<NonNull<T>>` has the same size as `*mut T`."},{"DocComment":" However the pointer may still dangle if it isn't dereferenced."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Unlike `*mut T`, `NonNull<T>` is covariant over `T`. This is usually the correct"},{"DocComment":" choice for most data structures and safe abstractions, such as `Box`, `Rc`, `Arc`, `Vec`,"},{"DocComment":" and `LinkedList`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In rare cases, if your type exposes a way to mutate the value of `T` through a `NonNull<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" and you need to prevent unsoundness from variance (for example, if `T` could be a reference"},{"DocComment":" with a shorter lifetime), you should add a field to make your type invariant, such as"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData<Cell<T>>` or `PhantomData<&'a mut T>`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Example of a type that must be invariant:"},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::Cell;"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" struct Invariant<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     ptr: std::ptr::NonNull<T>,"},{"DocComment":"     _invariant: PhantomData<Cell<T>>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Notice that `NonNull<T>` has a `From` instance for `&T`. However, this does"},{"DocComment":" not change the fact that mutating through a (pointer derived from a) shared"},{"DocComment":" reference is undefined behavior unless the mutation happens inside an"},{"DocComment":" [`UnsafeCell<T>`]. The same goes for creating a mutable reference from a shared"},{"DocComment":" reference. When using this `From` instance without an `UnsafeCell<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" it is your responsibility to ensure that `as_mut` is never called, and `as_ptr`"},{"DocComment":" is never used for mutation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Representation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Thanks to the [null pointer optimization],"},{"DocComment":" `NonNull<T>` and `Option<NonNull<T>>`"},{"DocComment":" are guaranteed to have the same size and alignment:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::ptr::NonNull;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<NonNull<i16>>(), size_of::<Option<NonNull<i16>>>());"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(align_of::<NonNull<i16>>(), align_of::<Option<NonNull<i16>>>());"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<NonNull<str>>(), size_of::<Option<NonNull<str>>>());"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(align_of::<NonNull<str>>(), align_of::<Option<NonNull<str>>>());"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [covariant]: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/subtyping.html"},{"DocComment":" [`PhantomData`]: crate::marker::PhantomData"},{"DocComment":" [`UnsafeCell<T>`]: crate::cell::UnsafeCell"},{"DocComment":" [null pointer optimization]: crate::option#representation"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"NonNull"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":13,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["PhantomData",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":24,"beg":{"line":811,"col":0},"end":{"line":811,"col":39}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Zero-sized type used to mark things that \"act like\" they own a `T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Adding a `PhantomData<T>` field to your type tells the compiler that your"},{"DocComment":" type acts as though it stores a value of type `T`, even though it doesn't"},{"DocComment":" really. This information is used when computing certain safety properties."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For a more in-depth explanation of how to use `PhantomData<T>`, please see"},{"DocComment":" [the Nomicon](../../nomicon/phantom-data.html)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # A ghastly note 👻👻👻"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Though they both have scary names, `PhantomData` and 'phantom types' are"},{"DocComment":" related, but not identical. A phantom type parameter is simply a type"},{"DocComment":" parameter which is never used. In Rust, this often causes the compiler to"},{"DocComment":" complain, and the solution is to add a \"dummy\" use by way of `PhantomData`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused lifetime parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Perhaps the most common use case for `PhantomData` is a struct that has an"},{"DocComment":" unused lifetime parameter, typically as part of some unsafe code. For"},{"DocComment":" example, here is a struct `Slice` that has two pointers of type `*const T`,"},{"DocComment":" presumably pointing into an array somewhere:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0392"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The intention is that the underlying data is only valid for the"},{"DocComment":" lifetime `'a`, so `Slice` should not outlive `'a`. However, this"},{"DocComment":" intent is not expressed in the code, since there are no uses of"},{"DocComment":" the lifetime `'a` and hence it is not clear what data it applies"},{"DocComment":" to. We can correct this by telling the compiler to act *as if* the"},{"DocComment":" `Slice` struct contained a reference `&'a T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # #[allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This also in turn infers the lifetime bound `T: 'a`, indicating"},{"DocComment":" that any references in `T` are valid over the lifetime `'a`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When initializing a `Slice` you simply provide the value"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData` for the field `phantom`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" # struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":" #     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" fn borrow_vec<T>(vec: &Vec<T>) -> Slice<'_, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     let ptr = vec.as_ptr();"},{"DocComment":"     Slice {"},{"DocComment":"         start: ptr,"},{"DocComment":"         end: unsafe { ptr.add(vec.len()) },"},{"DocComment":"         phantom: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused type parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It sometimes happens that you have unused type parameters which"},{"DocComment":" indicate what type of data a struct is \"tied\" to, even though that"},{"DocComment":" data is not actually found in the struct itself. Here is an"},{"DocComment":" example where this arises with [FFI]. The foreign interface uses"},{"DocComment":" handles of type `*mut ()` to refer to Rust values of different"},{"DocComment":" types. We track the Rust type using a phantom type parameter on"},{"DocComment":" the struct `ExternalResource` which wraps a handle."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [FFI]: ../../book/ch19-01-unsafe-rust.html#using-extern-functions-to-call-external-code"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # trait ResType { }"},{"DocComment":" # struct ParamType;"},{"DocComment":" # mod foreign_lib {"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn new(_: usize) -> *mut () { 42 as *mut () }"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn do_stuff(_: *mut (), _: usize) {}"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" # fn convert_params(_: ParamType) -> usize { 42 }"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"    resource_handle: *mut (),"},{"DocComment":"    resource_type: PhantomData<R>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<R: ResType> ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn new() -> Self {"},{"DocComment":"         let size_of_res = size_of::<R>();"},{"DocComment":"         Self {"},{"DocComment":"             resource_handle: foreign_lib::new(size_of_res),"},{"DocComment":"             resource_type: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"         }"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":"     fn do_stuff(&self, param: ParamType) {"},{"DocComment":"         let foreign_params = convert_params(param);"},{"DocComment":"         foreign_lib::do_stuff(self.resource_handle, foreign_params);"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Ownership and the drop check"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The exact interaction of `PhantomData` with drop check **may change in the future**."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Currently, adding a field of type `PhantomData<T>` indicates that your type *owns* data of type"},{"DocComment":" `T` in very rare circumstances. This in turn has effects on the Rust compiler's [drop check]"},{"DocComment":" analysis. For the exact rules, see the [drop check] documentation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For all `T`, the following are guaranteed:"},{"DocComment":" * `size_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 0`"},{"DocComment":" * `align_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 1`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [drop check]: Drop#drop-check"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"phantom_data"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":0,"align":1,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,{"def_id":15,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["array",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["IntoIter",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":32,"beg":{"line":20,"col":0},"end":{"line":20,"col":38}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A by-value [array] iterator."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"ArrayIntoIter"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[{"index":0,"name":"N","ty":{"Deduplicated":854}}],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":16,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["num",0]},{"Ident":["nonzero",0]},{"Ident":["NonZero",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":34,"beg":{"line":128,"col":0},"end":{"line":128,"col":40}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A value that is known not to equal zero."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This enables some memory layout optimization."},{"DocComment":" For example, `Option<NonZero<u32>>` is the same size as `u32`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use core::{num::NonZero};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<Option<NonZero<u32>>>(), size_of::<u32>());"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `NonZero<T>` is guaranteed to have the same layout and bit validity as `T`"},{"DocComment":" with the exception that the all-zero bit pattern is invalid."},{"DocComment":" `Option<NonZero<T>>` is guaranteed to be compatible with `T`, including in"},{"DocComment":" FFI."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Thanks to the [null pointer optimization], `NonZero<T>` and"},{"DocComment":" `Option<NonZero<T>>` are guaranteed to have the same size and alignment:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::num::NonZero;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<NonZero<u32>>(), size_of::<Option<NonZero<u32>>>());"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(align_of::<NonZero<u32>>(), align_of::<Option<NonZero<u32>>>());"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [null pointer optimization]: crate::option#representation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Note on generic usage"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `NonZero<T>` can only be used with some standard library primitive types"},{"DocComment":" (such as `u8`, `i32`, and etc.). The type parameter `T` must implement the"},{"DocComment":" internal trait [`ZeroablePrimitive`], which is currently permanently unstable"},{"DocComment":" and cannot be implemented by users. Therefore, you cannot use `NonZero<T>`"},{"DocComment":" with your own types, nor can you implement traits for all `NonZero<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" only for concrete types."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"NonZero"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"},{"index":1,"name":"Clause0_NonZeroInner"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":17,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["step_by",0]},{"Ident":["StepBy",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":36,"beg":{"line":16,"col":0},"end":{"line":16,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" An iterator for stepping iterators by a custom amount."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This `struct` is created by the [`step_by`] method on [`Iterator`]. See"},{"DocComment":" its documentation for more."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`step_by`]: Iterator::step_by"},{"DocComment":" [`Iterator`]: trait.Iterator.html"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"I"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":18,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["zip",0]},{"Ident":["Zip",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":37,"beg":{"line":13,"col":0},"end":{"line":13,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" An iterator that iterates two other iterators simultaneously."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This `struct` is created by [`zip`] or [`Iterator::zip`]."},{"DocComment":" See their documentation for more."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"A"},{"index":1,"name":"B"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":19,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["intersperse",0]},{"Ident":["Intersperse",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":38,"beg":{"line":9,"col":0},"end":{"line":9,"col":35}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" An iterator adapter that places a separator between all elements."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This `struct` is created by [`Iterator::intersperse`]. See its documentation"},{"DocComment":" for more information."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"I"},{"index":1,"name":"Clause0_Item"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":20,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["intersperse",0]},{"Ident":["IntersperseWith",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":38,"beg":{"line":83,"col":0},"end":{"line":83,"col":32}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" An iterator adapter that places a separator between all elements."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This `struct` is created by [`Iterator::intersperse_with`]. See its"},{"DocComment":" documentation for more information."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"I"},{"index":1,"name":"G"},{"index":2,"name":"Clause0_Item"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":21,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["iter",0]},{"Ident":["adapters",0]},{"Ident":["map",0]},{"Ident":["Map",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":39,"beg":{"line":61,"col":0},"end":{"line":61,"col":20}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" An iterator that maps the values of `iter` with `f`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This `struct` is created by the [`map`] method on [`Iterator`]. See its"},{"DocComment":" documentation for more."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`map`]: Iterator::map"},{"DocComment":" [`Iterator`]: trait.Iterator.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Notes about side effects"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The [`map`] iterator implements [`DoubleEndedIterator`], meaning that"},{"DocComment":" you can also [`map`] backwards:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" let v: Vec<i32> = [1, 2, 3].into_iter().map(|x| x + 1).rev().collect();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(v, [4, 3, 2]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`DoubleEndedIterator`]: trait.DoubleEndedIterator.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" But if your closure has state, iterating backwards may act in a way you do"},{"DocComment":" not expect. Let's go through an example. First, in the forward direction:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" let mut c = 0;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" for pair in ['a', 'b', 'c'].into_iter()"},{"DocComment":"                                .map(|letter| { c += 1; (letter, c) }) {"},{"DocComment":"     println!(\"{pair:?}\");"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This will print `('a', 1), ('b', 2), ('c', 3)`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Now consider this twist where we add a call to `rev`. This version will"},{"DocComment":" print `('c', 1), ('b', 2), ('a', 3)`. Note that the letters are reversed,"},{"DocComment":" but the values of the counter still go in order. This is because `map()` is"},{"DocComment":" still being called lazily on each item, but we are popping items off the"},{"DocComment":" back of the vector now, instead of shifting them from the front."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" let mut c = 0;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" for pair in ['a', 'b', 'c'].into_iter()"},{"DocComment":"                                .map(|letter| { c += 1; (letter, c) })"},{"DocComment":"                                .rev() {"},{"DocComment":"     println!(\"{pair:?}\");"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0